\ User-code reproducers for the preserved-bounded-row layout. Each word calls a
\ row-variable combinator on its own quotation parameter -- which collapses the
\ abstract stack to a bounded row -- and then operates on values threaded above
\ that row. The preserved layout keeps those values live, so the bodies compile
\ to native C under runtime-image AOT and produce the same results as the
\ interpreter. A combinator called on a literal quotation is specialized
\ instead, so the parameter is what forces the row.

\ keep-then-shuffle: `keep` runs the quotation parameter on a copy and leaves
\ its result above the threaded value; `swap` then reaches across the bounded
\ row to reorder them. This is the `1check` shape.
check-with: ( x quot -- ? x ) [ keep swap ] ;

\ counted loop: thread a counter and quotation above a bounded row introduced by
\ `2dip`, decrement the counter across the row, and recurse. The body nets zero
\ stack change across the row, so the back-edge invariant holds.
ntimes: ( n quot -- ) [
  over 0 >
  [ dup 2dip swap -- swap ntimes ]
  [ 2drop ]
  if
] ;

5 [ 2 > ] check-with . .
"--" print-line
3 [ "tick" print-line ] ntimes
